"""A pool of git worktrees backed by Bazel output bases that stay warm.

Bazel keys its server on `--workspace_directory`, so a worktree at a fresh path
gets a cold server: a new JVM, loading and analysis from zero, and a fresh
`external/` tree. Creating a worktree per task — which is what coding agents do
— pays that every time and strands the output base afterwards: Bazel has no
garbage collector for them, so one sits there until `aspect gc` or a person
removes it.

The fix is to stop letting the path move. A **slot** is a directory at a fixed
path that holds a git worktree; the worktree inside it is destroyed and
recreated as tasks come and go, but the path never changes, so the Bazel server
bound to it survives — and so does its output base, which Bazel keys on that
same path, with no flag from us.

A slot is where you work, at its own path. `git worktree list` names it,
`aspect worktree list` maps it to a branch, and `aspect worktree path <branch>`
prints it for `cd`. Nothing outside git points at the worktree — no symlink, no
injected flag — so nothing is left dangling if someone falls back to managing
worktrees by hand; the registry below is bookkeeping git overrides.

    ~/.aspect/worktrees/<pool>/aspect-worktree-a1b2c3
                                           (the git worktree; path never moves)

**Pools are keyed by the normalized `origin` remote**, not the clone path, so
every clone of a repository shares one pool directory. The slots inside it are
not shared: a slot records the git-common-dir of the clone that owns it, because
`git worktree add` is clone-scoped, and only that clone can lease or reconcile
it. A clone that moves, or is deleted, leaves its slots recorded for a path
that no longer has it, and `adopt_stranded` hands them on: a free slot to any
clone of the repository, a leased one to the clone whose git still lists its
checkout — the moved clone — whatever is now at the recorded path. What no
clone can take, a leased slot whose clone was deleted, is stranded: deleted by
naming it to `prune`, with `--force=all` since its checkout cannot be read —
from any clone of the repository, or by slot id from outside one.

**Git is the source of truth; the registry is a cache.** A slot whose worktree
git no longer knows about is free, whatever the registry says. That recovers
from a `git worktree remove` run by hand, and from an agent that deleted its
worktree without releasing the slot. Correctness never depends on `release`
being called.

What it does not recover from on its own is an agent that died with its checkout
still in place: git still knows about that worktree, so the lease stands until
somebody needs the slot. A stopped process alone is not enough — a harness
process exits whenever someone closes a terminal, and the session is resumable
— so the lease is given up only when a slot is wanted: when the alternative is
growing the pool, or when `add` asks for the very branch it holds.
`abandoned_lease` is what is safe to take back: the holder's process has
verifiably stopped (a holder with no pid recorded never has), the grace period
(`Worktrees.abandoned_grace_hours`) has passed since the slot's last activity,
so a session that stopped has time to come back, and the checkout is clean. The
clean part never bends — there is nothing uncommitted to lose, and the branch
and its commits survive removing a worktree.

**The lock covers deciding, not checking out.** Allocation picks a slot and
records a *reservation* on it under the pool lock, then drops the lock before
`git worktree add` — much the slowest thing it does — and retakes it only to
turn the reservation into a lease. Two agents starting together wait on each
other's bookkeeping rather than on each other's checkout. Both the lock and a
reservation record the process behind them, so one abandoned by a crash is
reclaimed at once instead of waited out, and a release that arrives late cannot
drop a lock somebody else now holds.

**The pool is bounded by pruning, not by refusing work.** Allocation reuses any
free slot the clone owns, however badly it scores — short of one holding work,
one whose directory will not go, and one a `score_slot` hook vetoes while the
pool is under `max_slots` — because a bad reuse costs at worst what a cold slot
costs and usually far less — the same refetch, minus a
new JVM, from-zero analysis and one more stranded base. New slots therefore
appear only when every owned slot is busy, and the pool settles at that clone's
peak concurrency. `max_slots` is advisory for the same reason: past it `add`
warns and proceeds, because failing on a bookkeeping budget would send the
caller back to plain `git worktree add`.

Disk is held down by idle time, but only for **free** slots: `add` drops those
nothing has touched in `PRUNE_IDLE_DAYS`, after adopting what a moved or deleted
clone left, and `aspect worktree prune` does the same on demand. A leased slot is never taken on idle time alone, however long,
because a lease is a checkout someone may still want. The one exception is
`abandoned_lease`.

Nothing here is specific to a developer machine. A CI runner that builds one
throwaway checkout per job has no use for a pool, but nothing is injected into
Bazel to make a slot work — a slot is a `git worktree add` at a stable path — so
there is nothing to conflict with on a runner that does want one.
"""

load("@std//hash.axl", "md5", "sha256")
load("@std//time.axl", "time")
load("./agent.axl", "is_running")
load("./bazel_paths.axl", "last_used", "output_user_root")
load("./environment.axl", "warn")
load("./fs_tree.axl", "remove_tree")
load("./process_info.axl", "alive", "is_same_process", "started_at")

_REGISTRY_VERSION = 1

# What a slot's `state` can be. `RESERVED` is the window where a slot has been
# claimed for a branch but its worktree is still being checked out, which is the
# whole reason the pool lock can be released for the slow part of `add`.
FREE = "free"
LEASED = "leased"
RESERVED = "reserved"

# Slot directory names carry this prefix. Public because `aspect gc` recognises
# a pool's output bases by the workspace they were built for, which is one of
# these, and a second copy of the spelling would be free to drift.
SLOT_PREFIX = "aspect-worktree-"

# Removing a pool worktree always passes `--force` twice. Once overrides a dirty
# tree; only twice overrides a *lock*, and every leased slot holds one (see
# `lock_reason`), as does a checkout `git worktree add` was killed in the middle
# of. Twice also unregisters a locked worktree whose directory is already gone,
# which `git worktree prune` never does.
FORCE_REMOVE = ["worktree", "remove", "--force", "--force"]

# The registry's filename. Named because `pools_on_disk` recognizes a pool by
# it, and a second spelling would be free to drift from the one that writes it.
_REGISTRY_NAME = "registry.json"

# Lock acquisition: a lock directory is staged with its owner and renamed into
# place, and a rename onto a lock that exists fails, so one that appears is a
# lock nobody else holds. Agents allocate concurrently, so this
# is load-bearing rather than defensive. Checkouts and `prune`'s confirmation
# run outside the lock; what happens under it is the registry bookkeeping and
# deleting the output bases of slots being pruned or aged out, which a minute
# usually outlasts. A caller still waiting after that gets `pool_busy`, which
# is retryable.
_LOCK_ATTEMPTS = 600
_LOCK_SLEEP_MS = 100

# A lock whose owner cannot be identified is given this long before it is taken
# to belong to a dead process. Only reached when the owner record is unreadable;
# an owner that can be read is checked directly, which needs no timeout at all.
# A lock is renamed into place with its owner already in it, so one without is
# left by an older CLI, made by hand, or caught part way through being removed.
_LOCK_STALE_MS = 10 * 1000

# A free slot nothing has touched for this long is worth less than the disk it
# holds: its server is long gone and its `external/` tree has drifted from every
# branch anyone is still working on. Set long rather than tight because the
# automatic pass inside `add` does not ask first — a month is past the life of
# any plausible branch, so reclaiming at that point surprises nobody.
PRUNE_IDLE_DAYS = 30

_DAY_MS = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000

# How many past leases a slot remembers. Enough to see what a slot has been
# holding lately, which is the question worth asking of it — a full history
# would grow the registry forever to answer nothing extra.
_HISTORY_LIMIT = 10

# A slot id: hex characters from a digest, as many as `list` shows, and how
# many times to draw one before deciding something is wrong.
_SLOT_ID_LENGTH = 12
_SLOT_ID_ATTEMPTS = 100

# Variables that point git at a repository other than the one `cwd` is in. Set
# around a git hook, or by a wrapper, they would make every question asked of a
# slot answer for somewhere else — and a clean answer from the wrong checkout
# is what licenses deleting the right one.
_GIT_LOCATION_VARS = [
    "GIT_DIR",
    "GIT_WORK_TREE",
    "GIT_INDEX_FILE",
    "GIT_COMMON_DIR",
    "GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY",
    "GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES",
    "GIT_NAMESPACE",
    "GIT_PREFIX",
    "GIT_QUARANTINE_PATH",
    "GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS",
    "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT",
    "GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES",
    "GIT_DISCOVERY_ACROSS_FILESYSTEM",
    "GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE",
    "GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS",
]

def run_git(std, args: list[str], cwd: str = "") -> struct:
    """Run `git <args>` and report the outcome rather than raising.

    Callers here treat a non-zero git as data — a directory that is not a
    repository, a clone with no `origin`, a branch that does not exist yet — so
    whether a failure is fatal is the caller's decision, not this one's. Git
    runs in `cwd` with `_GIT_LOCATION_VARS` removed from its environment, so
    the repository asked about is the one `cwd` is in. `code` is git's exit
    status, -1 where git could not be started at all — a `cwd` that a moved
    clone took with it, say.
    """
    cmd = std.process.command("git").args(args).stdout("piped").stderr("piped")
    for var in _GIT_LOCATION_VARS:
        cmd = cmd.env(var, None)
    if cwd:
        cmd = cmd.current_dir(cwd)
    err, child = catch(cmd.spawn)
    if err != None:
        return struct(ok = False, out = "", err = str(err), code = -1)
    out = child.wait_with_output()

    # Trailing whitespace only: `git status --porcelain` puts a meaningful space
    # at the start of a line — ` M` is a change not yet staged — and the path
    # starts at a fixed column after it.
    return struct(ok = out.status.code == 0, out = out.stdout.rstrip(), err = out.stderr.strip(), code = out.status.code)

def git_out(std, args: list[str], cwd: str = "") -> str:
    """`git <args>` stdout, trimmed; "" on any failure."""
    r = run_git(std, args, cwd)
    return r.out if r.ok else ""

# What `uncommitted_work` reports for a checkout `git status` fails in, shaped
# as a porcelain line so it lists, refuses and serializes like any other.
UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT = "UR (git cannot read this checkout)"

# Files a build generates that nobody authored: untracked, one is Bazel's and
# not work. One git tracks is not untracked — a change to it shows as a
# modification, a diff somebody may want to commit, and counts.
_GENERATED_FILES = ["MODULE.bazel.lock"]

def _is_bazel_leaving(std, worktree: str, base: str, entry: str) -> bool:
    """Whether an untracked path in a worktree is something Bazel put there.

    A convenience symlink is recognized by where it points — into this slot's
    own output base — rather than by its name, so a repo that sets
    `--symlink_prefix` is covered without naming its prefix.
    """
    if entry in _GENERATED_FILES:
        return True
    if not base or "/" in entry:
        return False
    err, meta = catch(std.fs.symlink_metadata, worktree + "/" + entry)
    if err != None or not meta.is_symlink:
        return False
    err, target = catch(std.fs.read_link, worktree + "/" + entry)
    return err == None and str(target).startswith(base)

def is_directory(std, path: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` exists and is a directory. `std.fs.is_dir` fails on a
    missing path rather than answering no."""
    return std.fs.exists(path) and std.fs.is_dir(path)

def _is_checkout(std, path: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` is a directory with a `.git` in it. A slot path replaced
    by a plain file is not, and asking for `<file>/.git` is an error rather
    than a no."""
    return is_directory(std, path) and std.fs.exists(path + "/.git")

def is_hex(text: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `text` is lowercase hex, as git prints object ids and as slot
    ids are made. False for "", which names nothing."""
    return text != "" and all([ch in "0123456789abcdef" for ch in text.elems()])

def _is_object_id(text: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `text` is a whole object id, SHA-1 or SHA-256, other than the
    null id a reflog's first entry carries."""
    return len(text) in (40, 64) and is_hex(text) and text.strip("0") != ""

def _head_reflog(std, worktree: str) -> list | None:
    """This worktree's HEAD reflog as `(commit, message)` pairs, or None when
    it exists and cannot be read — which a caller must take as work.

    Asked of git, which reads either ref backend, files or reftable. Git
    refuses on an unborn branch, after `git switch --orphan`, which is exactly
    where a detached HEAD's commits are easiest to leave behind; there the
    files backend's `logs/HEAD` is read directly, each line's old and new
    commit both. Newest first either way. A reftable reflog has no such file, and is then unreadable."""
    listed = run_git(std, ["log", "-g", "--format=%H %gs", "HEAD", "--"], worktree)
    if listed.ok:
        return [(line.partition(" ")[0], line.partition(" ")[2]) for line in listed.out.splitlines() if line]
    where = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-path", "logs/HEAD"], worktree)
    raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(where) if where and std.fs.exists(where) else None
    if raw == None:
        # Only git saying in so many words that there is no reflog means none;
        # a reftable one it cannot list here, or a git that cannot run at all,
        # is a reflog nobody read.
        return [] if run_git(std, ["reflog", "exists", "HEAD"], worktree).code == 1 else None

    # Newest first, as `git log -g` lists it: the file is oldest first.
    entries = []
    for line in reversed(raw.splitlines()):
        head, _, message = line.partition("\t")
        fields = head.split(" ")
        if len(fields) >= 2:
            entries.append((fields[1], message))
            entries.append((fields[0], message))
    return entries

def _reflog_candidates(std, worktree: str) -> list[str] | None:
    """The current HEAD when detached, whatever the branch reflogs hold — the
    reflog has it too, unless reflogs are off or were expired — and every
    other commit this worktree's HEAD reflog records that no branch's reflog
    does: made at a detached HEAD, or left by `checkout`, `reset` or `rebase`.

    A branch's own reflog keeps what it left behind, an amend or a rebase, so
    those are the branch owner's choice and not counted. A rebase's steps
    count like any other entry — a rebase at a detached HEAD has its result
    nowhere else — except the run of step entries directly under a rebase's
    finish or abort, which are skipped: a finish put the result on the branch,
    and an abort is the decision to drop them. The run ends at the first entry
    that is not a step, so nothing made meanwhile — a commit or an amend at a
    stop — is ever skipped, and a start that was not recognised costs a false
    refusal rather than a lost commit. An aborted `git am`'s own entries are
    skipped down to the commit it returned to. A commit HEAD only
    moved to, by `checkout`, also counts as kept when a remote-tracking
    branch's reflog has it: a pull request reviewed at a detached HEAD, then
    force-pushed over, was never only here. One made here is not excused that
    way, since a remote's reflog is gone with the next pruning fetch. A branch
    deleted with `-D` takes its reflog with it, so its commits count here: no
    ref reaches them either. None when the reflog exists and could not be
    read."""
    if not _is_checkout(std, worktree):
        return []
    return _candidates_at(std, worktree)

def _candidates_at(std, cwd: str) -> list[str] | None:
    """`_reflog_candidates`, asked of git in `cwd`: a checkout, or the admin
    directory git keeps for a worktree, which answers for its HEAD, its reflog
    and its refs as the checkout would."""
    worktree = cwd
    on_branches = {}
    for line in git_out(std, ["log", "-g", "--format=%H", "--branches", "--"], worktree).splitlines():
        on_branches[line] = True
    on_remotes = {}
    for line in git_out(std, ["log", "-g", "--format=%H", "--remotes", "--"], worktree).splitlines():
        on_remotes[line] = True
    found = []
    seen = {}
    if not run_git(std, ["symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "HEAD"], worktree).ok:
        head = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "HEAD"], worktree)
        if head:
            found.append(head)
            seen[head] = True
    reflog = _head_reflog(std, worktree)
    if reflog == None:
        return None

    # Newest first, so a rebase's end is met before the steps it ended. Only
    # the unbroken run of step entries under it is skipped: whatever is not a
    # step ends the run, and is counted.
    in_rebase = False
    am_back = None
    for commit, message in reflog:
        # The action ends at the first ": ". A pull from a path holding ": "
        # leaves its steps unrecognised, and so counted.
        action = message.partition(": ")[0]
        step = _sequencer_step(action)
        if in_rebase:
            if step:
                in_rebase = step != "start"
                continue
            in_rebase = False
        if step == "abort":
            in_rebase = True
            continue
        if step == "finish":
            in_rebase = True

        # Only the entries of the session that was aborted: they stop at the
        # commit HEAD was at before it, which is kept. The fallback reader
        # lists each line twice, so the abort is taken from its first.
        if action == "am --abort":
            if am_back == None:
                am_back = commit
            continue
        if am_back != None:
            if action == "am" and commit != am_back:
                continue
            am_back = None

        moved = action == "checkout"
        ids = [commit]
        if message.startswith("checkout: moving from "):
            ids.append(message[len("checkout: moving from "):].split(" ")[0])
        for one in ids:
            if not _is_object_id(one) or one in seen or one in on_branches or (moved and one in on_remotes):
                continue
            seen[one] = True
            found.append(one)
    return found

# The steps a rebase writes to the reflog, whatever action names it.
_SEQUENCER_STEPS = ["start", "pick", "continue", "finish", "abort", "skip", "reword", "edit", "squash", "fixup", "reset", "label", "merge", "update-ref"]

def _sequencer_step(action: str) -> str:
    """The rebase step a reflog action records — `rebase (pick)`,
    `rebase -i (finish)`, `pull --rebase origin main (start)` — or "" for
    anything else. Only a `rebase` or `pull` action counts, with nothing in
    parentheses before its step: a merge of a branch named `x(abort)` is a
    merge. A tool's own `GIT_REFLOG_ACTION` is not recognised, so its entries
    are counted like any other."""
    if not action.endswith(")") or not (action.startswith("rebase") or action.startswith("pull")):
        return ""
    head, _, step = action.removesuffix(")").rpartition(" (")
    if "(" in head or ")" in head:
        return ""
    return step if step in _SEQUENCER_STEPS else ""

# Refs that keep a commit only for this worktree, and so go with it.
_PER_WORKTREE_REFS = ["refs/worktree/*", "refs/bisect/*", "refs/rewritten/*"]

def _unreachable(std, worktree: str, tips: list[str], own: str = "") -> list[str] | None:
    """The commits `tips` reach that nothing outliving this worktree does: no
    ref outside the per-worktree namespaces, and no other worktree's HEAD.
    None when git could not answer, which a caller must read as work rather
    than as none. `own` is the worktree's path where git is asked from
    elsewhere — its admin directory — so its own HEAD is not taken for
    another's."""
    if not tips:
        return []
    others = []
    current = ""
    here = real_path(std, own or worktree)
    listed = worktree_porcelain(std, worktree)
    for line in (listed.split("\0") if "\0" in listed else listed.splitlines()):
        if line.startswith("worktree "):
            current = line[len("worktree "):]
        elif line.startswith("HEAD ") and real_path(std, current) != here and _is_object_id(line[5:]):
            others.append(line[5:])
    args = ["rev-list", "--ignore-missing"] + tips + ["--not"]
    args = args + ["--exclude=" + glob for glob in _PER_WORKTREE_REFS] + ["--glob=refs/*"] + others
    listed = run_git(std, args, worktree)
    if not listed.ok:
        return None
    return listed.out.splitlines()

def commits_only(std, worktree: str, tips: list[str] | None, own: str = "") -> int:
    """How many commits `tips` reach that nothing outliving this worktree
    does (`_unreachable`, with `own`). -1 when git could not say, or `tips`
    could not be read, which counts as work."""
    commits = None if tips == None else _unreachable(std, worktree, tips, own)
    return -1 if commits == None else len(commits)

def unreferenced_tips(std, worktree: str) -> list[str] | None:
    """The fewest commits whose branches would keep everything removing the
    worktree, and its HEAD reflog with it, would leave dangling: the
    `_reflog_candidates` nothing else reaches, less those another of them
    reaches. `git branch <name> <tip>` on each keeps the lot. Every candidate
    when git could not say which are lost; None when the reflog itself could
    not be read. Empty where there is no checkout."""
    candidates = _reflog_candidates(std, worktree)
    if candidates == None:
        return None
    commits = _unreachable(std, worktree, candidates)
    if commits == None:
        return candidates
    lost = {c: True for c in commits}
    tips = [tip for tip in candidates if tip in lost]
    if len(tips) < 2:
        return tips
    independent = run_git(std, ["merge-base", "--independent"] + tips, worktree)
    return independent.out.splitlines() if independent.ok and independent.out else tips

def unreferenced_commits(std, worktree: str) -> int:
    """How many commits only this worktree's HEAD reflog reaches — which
    removing the worktree leaves dangling. -1 when git could not say, which
    counts as work; 0 where there is no checkout."""
    return commits_only(std, worktree, _reflog_candidates(std, worktree))

# `git status` flags that no setting may weaken: untracked files are listed
# whatever `status.showUntrackedFiles` says, and a submodule's changes count
# whatever `submodule.<name>.ignore` says. `core.fsmonitor` is off so a stale
# monitor cannot report a tree clean. `core.quotePath` is off, so a name
# outside ASCII comes through as itself rather than as octal bytes, which a
# JSON consumer could not use; git still quotes one with a control character,
# a quote or a backslash, and the text report escapes what is left.
_STATUS = [
    "-c",
    "core.fsmonitor=false",
    "-c",
    "core.quotePath=false",
    "status",
    "--porcelain",
    "--untracked-files=normal",
    "--ignore-submodules=none",
]

def uncommitted_work(std, worktree: str, base: str = "") -> list[str]:
    """`git status --porcelain` lines for a worktree, minus Bazel's own leavings,
    plus `hidden_work`: what removing the checkout loses, short of commits.

    What a dirty check is for is protecting work, and build output is not work:
    the convenience symlinks point into the output base and the next build
    recreates them, and a generated lock file costs a re-resolution. Counting
    them would make any slot that had been built in impossible to release or to
    reclaim, which is every slot the pool is for.

    Empty for a worktree that is gone, or an empty directory. A checkout git
    cannot read — its clone moved, its index damaged, its `.git` deleted, a
    repository of its own at the path, a `.git` naming another worktree, or a
    `core.worktree` pointing elsewhere — reports one `UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT`
    entry rather than none: callers that delete do so on an empty answer, and
    work nobody could see is still work.
    """
    if not is_directory(std, worktree):
        return []
    if not std.fs.exists(worktree + "/.git"):
        err, entries = catch(std.fs.read_dir, worktree)
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + ": it has no .git"] if err != None or entries else []

    # A slot's checkout is a linked worktree, with a `.git` file. A `.git`
    # directory is a repository of its own put at the slot's path, whose
    # branches and history nothing here can vouch for.
    if is_directory(std, worktree + "/.git"):
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + ": it is a repository of its own, not a worktree of this clone"]
    status = run_git(std, _STATUS, worktree)
    if not status.ok:
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + (": " + status.err.splitlines()[0] if status.err else "")]
    elsewhere = _linked_elsewhere(std, worktree)
    if elsewhere:
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + ": its .git names the worktree at " + elsewhere + ", so git reads that one's state, not this"]
    top = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], worktree)
    if top and real_path(std, top) != real_path(std, worktree):
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + ": git's work tree for it is " + top + " (`core.worktree`), so its state says nothing about this directory"]
    kept = []
    for line in status.out.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("?? ") and _is_bazel_leaving(std, worktree, base, line[3:].strip()):
            continue
        if line:
            kept.append(line)
    return kept + hidden_work(std, worktree)

def _linked_elsewhere(std, worktree: str) -> str:
    """The checkout a linked worktree's `.git` actually names, when that is not
    `worktree`, else "". A `.git` file copied or edited to point at another
    worktree's admin directory makes git answer every question about that one
    — its HEAD, its reflog, its index — so nothing seen through it is this
    checkout's."""
    admin = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir"], worktree)
    common = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"], worktree)
    if not admin or admin == common:
        return ""
    back = (std.fs.try_read_to_string(admin + "/gitdir") or "").strip()
    if not back:
        return ""

    # Relative where `worktree.useRelativePaths` wrote it: to the admin directory.
    if not back.startswith("/"):
        back = admin + "/" + back
    named = back.removesuffix("/.git")
    return "" if real_path(std, named) == real_path(std, worktree) else named

def _admin_dir(std, git_common_dir: str, path: str) -> str:
    """The directory git keeps for the worktree at `path` —
    `<common>/worktrees/<name>`, holding its HEAD, reflog and refs — or "".

    Paths are compared resolved, since git canonicalizes what it records, but
    only for an entry whose last segment is the same: resolving a path does not
    rename the directory itself, and resolving every entry for every slot asked
    about spawns a process per pair."""
    err, entries = catch(std.fs.read_dir, git_common_dir + "/worktrees")
    if err != None:
        return ""
    path = path.rstrip("/")
    leaf = path.rpartition("/")[2]
    want = None
    for entry in entries:
        admin = git_common_dir + "/worktrees/" + entry.path.rpartition("/")[2]
        back = (std.fs.try_read_to_string(admin + "/gitdir") or "").strip()
        if not back:
            continue

        # Relative where `worktree.useRelativePaths` wrote it: to the admin directory.
        if not back.startswith("/"):
            back = admin + "/" + back
        named = back.removesuffix("/.git")
        if named == path:
            return admin
        if named.rpartition("/")[2] != leaf:
            continue
        if want == None:
            want = real_path(std, path)
        if real_path(std, named) == want:
            return admin
    return ""

# The first line of `vanished_work`'s answer, which says what the rest is.
VANISHED_CHECKOUT = "VG (the checkout is gone from its directory; git still keeps this for it)"

def vanished_work(std, git_common_dir: str, path: str) -> list[str]:
    """What git still keeps for a worktree whose directory is gone or emptied,
    that removing its registration would lose: commits only its HEAD reflog
    reaches, refs only it has, its index's staged changes, and an operation
    in progress. Lines as `work_in` gives them, under a
    `VANISHED_CHECKOUT` line; empty when there is nothing, or no admin
    directory to ask.

    A slot moved away with `mv`, or deleted, still has its admin directory in
    the clone, and git's lock on a leased one keeps it there. That directory is
    the last thing reaching a detached HEAD's commits; dropping the
    registration — to free the slot or the branch — deletes it."""
    if not git_common_dir:
        return []
    admin = _admin_dir(std, git_common_dir, path)
    if not admin:
        # Listed by git with no admin directory found for it: what it keeps
        # cannot be read, which is not the same as nothing.
        if path in _live_worktrees(std, git_common_dir):
            return [VANISHED_CHECKOUT, "?? (git's admin directory for it could not be found)"]
        return []

    # Git asked from a directory with no HEAD file does not take it for a git
    # directory, and answers for the clone around it instead.
    if not std.fs.exists(admin + "/HEAD"):
        return [VANISHED_CHECKOUT, "?? (git's admin directory for it has no HEAD, so what it keeps cannot be read)"]
    found = []
    for ref in git_out(std, ["for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname)", "refs/worktree", "refs/bisect"], admin).splitlines():
        if ref:
            found.append("WR " + ref + "  (a ref only this worktree has)")

    # The index and an operation's state live here too, not in the checkout:
    # a slot moved mid-merge keeps its files, but its staged resolution and
    # MERGE_HEAD go with the registration.
    found += _in_progress(std, admin)

    # No index is nothing staged: a checkout cut short before writing one, which
    # `diff --cached` would report as everything staged for deletion.
    staged = run_git(std, ["diff", "--cached", "--quiet"], admin).code if std.fs.exists(admin + "/index") else 0
    if staged == 1:
        found.append("IX (changes staged in its index, which lives with the registration)")
    elif staged != 0:
        found.append("IX (whether its index holds staged changes, which git could not say)")
    candidates = _candidates_at(std, admin)
    lost = _unreachable(std, admin, candidates, own = path) if candidates != None else None
    if lost == None:
        found.append("DH (commits only this worktree's HEAD reflog may reach, which git could not count)")
    elif lost:
        kept = {c: True for c in lost}
        tips = [c for c in candidates if c in kept]
        if len(tips) > 1:
            independent = run_git(std, ["merge-base", "--independent"] + tips, admin)
            tips = independent.out.splitlines() if independent.ok and independent.out else tips
        found.append("DH (%d commit%s only this worktree's HEAD reaches; `git branch <name> <tip>` from the clone, for %s, keeps %s)" % (
            len(lost),
            "" if len(lost) == 1 else "s",
            ("tip " + tips[0]) if len(tips) == 1 else ("each of " + ", ".join(tips[:5]) + (
                " and %d more" % (len(tips) - 5) if len(tips) > 5 else ""
            )),
            "it" if len(lost) == 1 else "them",
        ))
    return [VANISHED_CHECKOUT] + found if found else []

def work_in(std, worktree: str, base: str = "", git_common_dir: str = "") -> list[str]:
    """Everything in a checkout that removing it would lose, as porcelain-shaped
    lines: `uncommitted_work`, and commits only this worktree's HEAD reaches. Empty
    means the checkout can go without asking. Files git ignores are not work:
    whatever a repository ignores is by its own account disposable.

    The one check every path that deletes a checkout unasked goes through, so
    none of them can forget a kind of work the others count. Where the
    checkout's directory is gone, it is `vanished_work` — what git keeps for it
    in `git_common_dir`'s admin directory — which only a caller naming the
    clone can ask; without one, a missing directory holds nothing it can see.
    So is one with no `.git` in it, emptied or recreated.
    A file where the checkout should be is `UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT`: somebody's,
    not debris.
    """
    if std.fs.exists(worktree) and not is_directory(std, worktree):
        return [UNREADABLE_CHECKOUT + ": a file is at the slot's path, not a checkout"]
    if not is_directory(std, worktree):
        return vanished_work(std, git_common_dir, worktree) if git_common_dir else []

    # A directory with no `.git` — emptied, or recreated by hand — is a
    # checkout gone from it as much as a missing one is, and git's admin
    # directory for it keeps the same things.
    if not std.fs.exists(worktree + "/.git"):
        return uncommitted_work(std, worktree, base) + (vanished_work(std, git_common_dir, worktree) if git_common_dir else [])
    found = uncommitted_work(std, worktree, base)
    orphans = unreferenced_commits(std, worktree)
    if orphans < 0:
        found = found + ["DH (commits only this worktree's HEAD reflog may reach, which git could not count)"]
    elif orphans:
        found = found + ["DH (%d commit%s only this worktree's HEAD reaches)" % (orphans, "" if orphans == 1 else "s")]
    return found

def hidden_work(std, worktree: str) -> list[str]:
    """Work in a checkout that `git status` does not report, as porcelain-shaped
    lines: a populated submodule, whose repository lives inside this worktree's
    git directory and goes with it; files hidden from status by
    `--assume-unchanged` or `--skip-worktree` that are on disk; refs
    only this worktree has (`refs/worktree/`, `refs/bisect/`); a merge,
    rebase, cherry-pick or bisect in progress; and another worktree git lists
    inside this one — under an ignored directory, `.claude/worktrees/` say —
    which removing this one deletes, its own changes and HEAD reflog with it.

    Counted as work, so removing such a checkout takes the same `--force` as
    uncommitted changes — as `git worktree remove` refuses a worktree with
    submodules at all.
    """
    found = []

    # From the index rather than `git submodule status`, which fails outright
    # for a gitlink `.gitmodules` does not map — an embedded repository whose
    # commits would go with the worktree all the same.
    for entry in git_out(std, ["ls-files", "--stage", "-z"], worktree).split("\0"):
        meta, _, path = entry.partition("\t")
        if meta.startswith("160000 ") and std.fs.exists(worktree + "/" + path + "/.git"):
            found.append("SM " + path + "  (a submodule, whose repository is inside this worktree)")

    # A skip-worktree file on disk is hidden whether or not the checkout is
    # sparse: git un-skips one it finds present, unless
    # `sparse.expectFilesOutsideOfPatterns` tells it not to.
    for line in git_out(std, ["ls-files", "-v", "-z"], worktree).split("\0"):
        tag, _, path = line.partition(" ")
        hidden = (tag.islower() and tag != "") or tag == "S"
        if hidden and std.fs.exists(worktree + "/" + path):
            found.append("H  " + path + "  (hidden from git status by --assume-unchanged or --skip-worktree)")

    for ref in git_out(std, ["for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname)", "refs/worktree", "refs/bisect"], worktree).splitlines():
        if ref:
            found.append("WR " + ref + "  (a ref only this worktree has)")

    git_dir = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir"], worktree)
    if git_dir:
        found += _in_progress(std, git_dir)

    here = real_path(std, worktree)
    for path in _live_worktrees(std, worktree):
        for root in [here, worktree]:
            if path.startswith(root + "/") and is_directory(std, path):
                found.append("NW " + path[len(root) + 1:] + "  (a worktree nested in this one, which removing it takes too)")
                break
    return found

# What a worktree's git directory holds while an operation is half done.
_IN_PROGRESS = [
    ("rebase-merge", "a rebase"),
    ("rebase-apply", "a rebase or am"),
    ("MERGE_HEAD", "a merge"),
    ("CHERRY_PICK_HEAD", "a cherry-pick"),
    ("REVERT_HEAD", "a revert"),
    ("BISECT_LOG", "a bisect"),
    ("sequencer", "a cherry-pick or revert sequence"),
]

def _in_progress(std, git_dir: str) -> list[str]:
    """`IP` lines for each operation half done in a worktree's git directory."""
    return [
        "IP " + marker + "  (" + what + " in progress)"
        for marker, what in _IN_PROGRESS
        if std.fs.exists(git_dir + "/" + marker)
    ]

def normalize_remote(url: str) -> str:
    """A remote URL reduced to `host/path`, as a pool key.

    The same repository is spelled at least three ways — `git@host:path.git`,
    `ssh://git@host/path`, `https://host/path` — and a pool key has to be stable
    across them. Mirrors on different hosts are deliberately NOT unified: a
    GitHub and a GitLab URL for the same code become separate pools, because
    guessing that two hosts serve one repository is a worse failure than keeping
    two pools.

    "" for a remote that is not a host and a path at all — a filesystem path, a
    `file://` URL, or anything with a `..` in it. Those have no host to key on,
    and a key that began with a separator or climbed out of the pools root would
    compose into a directory the caller could not then recognize. The caller
    keys those on a slug instead.
    """
    s = url.strip()
    if not s:
        return ""
    had_scheme = False
    for prefix in ["ssh://", "https://", "http://", "git://", "file://"]:
        if s.startswith(prefix):
            s = s[len(prefix):]
            had_scheme = True
            break
    if not had_scheme:
        # scp-like `user@host:path`, the only form with a colon separating the
        # host from a path that does not start with `/`.
        at = s.find("@")
        colon = s.find(":")
        if colon > 0 and (at < 0 or at < colon):
            s = s[:colon] + "/" + s[colon + 1:]
    if s.startswith("/") or s.startswith(".") or s.startswith("~"):
        return ""

    at = s.find("@")
    slash = s.find("/")
    if at > 0 and (slash < 0 or at < slash):
        s = s[at + 1:]

    # Trailing slashes come off first, so `https://host/path.git/` reduces the
    # same way `https://host/path.git` does.
    s = s.rstrip("/")
    if s.endswith(".git"):
        s = s[:-len(".git")]
    key = s.rstrip("/").lower()
    if ".." in key.split("/"):
        return ""
    return key

def slug(s: str) -> str:
    """Arbitrary text as a lowercase, filesystem-safe token.

    Used for a git dir standing in as a pool key, so it has to be total:
    anything outside `[a-z0-9-]` becomes a dash, and leading and trailing
    dashes come off the result.
    """
    out = ""
    for ch in s.elems():
        if (ch >= "a" and ch <= "z") or (ch >= "0" and ch <= "9") or ch == "-":
            out += ch
        elif ch >= "A" and ch <= "Z":
            out += ch.lower()
        else:
            out += "-"
    return out.strip("-") or "worktree"

def pools_root_of(home: str) -> str:
    """Where every pool lives, under an explicit home directory.

    Separate from `pools_root` because a caller matching a path against the pool
    root has to try more than one spelling of home: a symlinked `/Users` and
    `/System/Volumes/Data/Users` name the same directory but do not compare
    equal.
    """
    return home + "/.aspect/worktrees"

def pools_root(std) -> str:
    """Where every pool lives. One place, so a reaper can see all of them.

    Under home with its symlinks resolved, because git reports worktree paths
    resolved, and every comparison between a slot path built here and one git
    lists has to see the same string.
    """
    return real_path(std, pools_root_of(std.env.home_dir()))

def real_path(std, path: str) -> str:
    """`path` with symlinks resolved, or as given where `realpath` cannot say.

    Resolved through its deepest existing directory, so a path not created yet
    — the pools root before the first slot — is spelled as it will be once it
    exists.
    """
    head = path
    tail = ""
    for _ in range(path.count("/")):
        if std.fs.exists(head):
            break
        head, _, last = head.rpartition("/")
        tail = "/" + last + tail
    out = std.process.command("realpath").args([head]).stdout("piped").stderr("null").spawn().wait_with_output()
    resolved = out.stdout.strip() if out.status.code == 0 else ""
    return (resolved + tail) if resolved else path

# How many path segments below the pools root a pool id may have. `host/org/repo`
# is three; a GitLab subgroup adds one or two. Past this a directory is not
# treated as a pool, which keeps a stray tree under the root from being walked
# forever.
_POOL_ID_DEPTH = 8

def pools_on_disk(std) -> list[dict]:
    """Every pool on this machine, ordered by id.

    A pool is a directory under the pools root containing a registry, at
    whatever depth — the id is the path below the root, and that is three
    segments for a normal remote and more for a nested group. The registry is
    the marker rather than the depth.

    `git_common_dir` is empty, because no clone was consulted: that is the whole
    point of listing pools a clone does not own, and leaving it empty is what
    stops `reconcile` from judging slots against the wrong object store. The
    caller supplies its own pool dict for the one it does own.
    """
    root = pools_root(std)
    if not std.fs.exists(root):
        return []

    found = []
    frontier = [""]
    for _ in range(_POOL_ID_DEPTH):
        deeper = []
        for rel in frontier:
            at = root + ("/" + rel if rel else "")
            err, entries = catch(std.fs.read_dir, at)
            if err != None:
                continue
            for entry in entries:
                if not entry.is_dir:
                    continue
                child = (rel + "/" + entry.path) if rel else entry.path
                if std.fs.exists(at + "/" + entry.path + "/" + _REGISTRY_NAME):
                    found.append(child)

                    # Not into what could be a slot or a lock: a pool that lost its
                    # registry still holds checkouts, whose contents are not pools.

                elif not entry.path.startswith(SLOT_PREFIX) and not entry.path.startswith(".lock"):
                    deeper.append(child)
        frontier = deeper
        if not frontier:
            break

    return [
        {"id": id, "dir": root + "/" + id, "git_common_dir": "", "remote": ""}
        for id in sorted(found)
    ]

def pool_of(std, cwd: str) -> dict:
    """The pool this directory belongs to, or an empty dict outside a repo.

    `git_common_dir` collapses every worktree of a clone onto one value, which
    is exactly the set of slots that clone can own.
    """
    common = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"], cwd)
    if not common:
        return {}
    origin = git_out(std, ["remote", "get-url", "origin"], cwd)
    pool_id = normalize_remote(origin)
    if not pool_id:
        # Either there is no origin, or it is a filesystem path rather than a
        # host and a path. Keying on a slug keeps every clone of the same local
        # repository in one pool and keeps the key free of separators; with no
        # origin at all the clone's own git dir is the most that can be said,
        # and `list` says so.
        pool_id = "local/" + slug(origin or common)
    return {
        "id": pool_id,
        "git_common_dir": common,
        "remote": origin,
        "dir": pools_root(std) + "/" + pool_id,
    }

def _digest(s: str) -> str:
    """Hex sha256 of a string. `sha256()` is a streaming hasher, not a one-shot
    function."""
    h = sha256()
    h.update(s)
    return h.hexdigest()

def _lock_dir(pool_dir: str) -> str:
    return pool_dir + "/.lock"

def _lock_owner_path(pool_dir: str) -> str:
    return _lock_dir(pool_dir) + "/owner.json"

def owner_record(std) -> dict:
    """This process, as the holder of a lock or a reservation.

    A pid on its own is ambiguous — pids are reused, and a recorded one outlives
    the process that wrote it — so the start time goes in beside it, which is
    exactly what `is_same_process` settles. `token` is the pair as one string,
    for the holder to prove it still owns what it took.
    """
    pid = std.process.id()
    start = started_at(std, pid)
    return {"token": "%d:%s" % (pid, start), "pid": pid, "started_at": start}

def _held_by_a_live_process(std, record: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the process in a lock or reservation record is still running.

    A record with no usable pid cannot vouch for anything, so it counts as gone:
    the alternative is state that nothing is allowed to clean up.
    """
    pid = record.get("pid", 0)
    if type(pid) != "int" or pid <= 0:
        return False
    return is_same_process(std, pid, record.get("started_at", ""))

def _lock_owner(std, pool_dir: str) -> dict:
    """Who holds the pool lock, or an empty dict if that cannot be read."""
    raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(_lock_owner_path(pool_dir))
    if not raw:
        return {}
    owner = json.try_decode(raw)
    return owner if type(owner) == "dict" else {}

def _lock_abandoned(std, pool_dir: str, owner: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the lock's holder is gone, so taking it from them loses nothing.

    `owner` is the record as the caller read it, so the lock judged is the lock
    a steal then compares against. The owner record answers directly: a holder
    whose process has exited is never coming back to release it, whatever the
    clock says. The directory's age is the fallback for a lock whose owner
    cannot be read — a process that died between creating the directory and
    recording itself in it. An age that cannot be established is never called
    abandoned, because reclaiming a live lock is worse than waiting.
    """
    if owner:
        return not _held_by_a_live_process(std, owner)

    # `modified` is MICROSECONDS since the epoch, and None where the platform
    # cannot report it.
    err, meta = catch(std.fs.metadata, _lock_dir(pool_dir))
    if err != None or meta.modified == None:
        return False
    return time.now_ms() - (meta.modified // 1000) > _LOCK_STALE_MS

def _take_lock(std, pool_dir: str) -> str:
    """Create the lock directory and record this process in it, returning the
    token that releases it, or "" if somebody else has it.

    The lock is a directory, made aside and renamed into place: renaming onto
    a lock that exists, owner and all, fails, which is the whole mechanism —
    two agents racing to allocate cannot both succeed.
    """

    # Built aside with its owner recorded, then renamed into place: a lock is
    # never seen without an owner, however long the write takes or whether it
    # fails. `rename` onto an existing lock fails, since it holds its owner.
    record = owner_record(std)
    staged = _lock_dir(pool_dir) + ".new-" + record["token"].replace(":", "-").replace(" ", "-")
    catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, staged)
    if catch(std.fs.create_dir, staged)[0] != None:
        return ""
    if catch(std.fs.write, staged + "/owner.json", json.encode(record))[0] != None:
        catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, staged)
        return ""
    if std.fs.exists(_lock_dir(pool_dir)) or catch(std.fs.rename, staged, _lock_dir(pool_dir))[0] != None:
        catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, staged)
        return ""
    return record["token"]

def _steal_lock(std, pool_dir: str, judged: str) -> bool:
    """Remove an abandoned lock, returning whether this caller did. `judged` is
    the owner token the caller found dead, "" for a lock judged by age.

    Removing in place races: two callers that both judged the old holder dead
    can each remove the lock, and the second removes the one the first has
    just taken. Renaming is atomic, so only one caller moves the lock aside;
    and unless what it moved is the very lock it judged — same owner token, or
    no owner for a lock judged by age — it is put back rather than removed: a
    live holder may have taken it in between, before or after recording itself.
    """
    aside = _lock_dir(pool_dir) + ".stale-" + owner_record(std)["token"].replace(":", "-").replace(" ", "-")
    if catch(std.fs.rename, _lock_dir(pool_dir), aside)[0] != None:
        return False
    moved = std.fs.try_read_to_string(aside + "/owner.json")
    record = json.try_decode(moved) if moved else None
    same = (record.get("token", "") == judged) if type(record) == "dict" else judged == ""
    if not same:
        if catch(std.fs.rename, aside, _lock_dir(pool_dir))[0] != None:
            warn(std, "moved a live worktree pool lock aside and could not put it back, at " + aside)
        return False
    catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, aside)
    return True

def _tidy_lock_leftovers(std, pool_dir: str) -> None:
    """Put back a live lock that a steal moved aside, and remove what dead
    processes left beside the lock.

    A steal that finds it moved a live holder's lock puts it back; one killed
    in between, or beaten to the empty place, leaves it aside, with its holder
    still at work. Restoring it before anyone takes the place keeps that holder
    the only one. Staged and moved-aside directories whose owner has exited are
    nobody's, and go.
    """
    err, entries = catch(std.fs.read_dir, pool_dir)
    if err != None:
        return
    mine = owner_record(std)["token"]
    for entry in entries:
        name = entry.path.rpartition("/")[2]
        if not (name.startswith(".lock.new-") or name.startswith(".lock.stale-")):
            continue
        path = pool_dir + "/" + name
        raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(path + "/owner.json")
        owner = json.try_decode(raw) if raw else None
        live = type(owner) == "dict" and _held_by_a_live_process(std, owner)
        ours = type(owner) == "dict" and owner.get("token", "") == mine

        # This process's own lock, moved aside, it has already let go of.
        if ours and name.startswith(".lock.stale-"):
            catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, path)
        elif live and name.startswith(".lock.stale-") and not std.fs.exists(_lock_dir(pool_dir)):
            catch(std.fs.rename, path, _lock_dir(pool_dir))
        elif not live:
            catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, path)

def acquire(std, pool_dir: str, attempts: int = _LOCK_ATTEMPTS) -> str:
    """Take the pool's lock and return the token that releases it, or "".

    The holder records itself inside the lock, so one left behind by a process
    that died is reclaimed the moment the next caller looks rather than waited
    out — the alternative is a pool that wedges on a crash. Reclaiming is
    progress, not contention, so it is followed by another attempt instead of
    costing one.
    """
    std.fs.create_dir_all(pool_dir)
    _tidy_lock_leftovers(std, pool_dir)
    for _ in range(attempts):
        token = _take_lock(std, pool_dir)
        if token:
            return token
        owner = _lock_owner(std, pool_dir)
        if _lock_abandoned(std, pool_dir, owner) and _steal_lock(std, pool_dir, owner.get("token", "")):
            warn(std, "worktree pool lock belonged to a process that is gone; reclaiming it")

            # A second caller can win the race to retake it, which is ordinary
            # contention from here on.
            token = _take_lock(std, pool_dir)
            if token:
                return token
            continue
        time.sleep(_LOCK_SLEEP_MS)
    return ""

def release(std, pool_dir: str, token: str) -> None:
    """Drop the lock, unless it has since passed to another caller.

    `add` releases the lock before checking a worktree out and takes it again
    afterwards, so a release can arrive when the lock directory belongs to
    somebody else — and removing theirs would put two callers in the registry at
    once. An empty token is a lock already dropped. An owner that cannot be read
    is assumed to be this caller, because the alternative is a pool nothing can
    unlock.
    """
    if not token:
        return
    owner = _lock_owner(std, pool_dir)
    if not owner or owner.get("token", "") == token:
        catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, _lock_dir(pool_dir))

    # A steal that moved this lock aside and did not put it back leaves it
    # there; it is this caller's to drop, not anybody's to restore.
    err, entries = catch(std.fs.read_dir, pool_dir)
    for entry in (entries if err == None else []):
        name = entry.path.rpartition("/")[2]
        if name.startswith(".lock.stale-"):
            raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(pool_dir + "/" + name + "/owner.json")
            aside = json.try_decode(raw) if raw else None
            if type(aside) == "dict" and aside.get("token", "") == token:
                catch(std.fs.remove_dir_all, pool_dir + "/" + name)

def registry_path(pool_dir: str) -> str:
    """Where a pool keeps its registry. The one place the file is named, for
    this library and for anything else that reads a pool."""
    return pool_dir + "/" + _REGISTRY_NAME

def _warn_once(std, about: str, message: str) -> None:
    """`warn`, once per process for `about`. The registry is read more than
    once per command, and the process environment is the one place a "said
    already" can live — which every child process inherits, so a nested
    `aspect` run says it no more than once either."""
    said = "ASPECT_WORKTREE_WARNED_" + _digest(about)[:16]
    if not std.env.var(said):
        std.env.set_var(said, "1")
        warn(std, message)

def read_registry(std, pool_dir: str) -> dict:
    """The pool's registry, or an empty one. Never fails: a registry that cannot
    be parsed is a cache we can rebuild, not state worth stopping for.

    One a newer CLI wrote — a higher `version` — is read as far as this version
    understands it and marked `read_only`, so `write_registry` never drops what
    this version does not know; a `version` that is not a positive int, which
    no CLI writes, is damage like any other. In a newer registry, entries in a state it does
    not know are kept for the same reason, and skipped by everything that acts
    on slots. In a current one they are dropped, with a warning.

    An empty answer says why: `absent` for a pool with no registry yet, and
    `unreadable` for a damaged one. A reader that would act on a slot's *absence*
    — `aspect gc` deciding nothing claims a base — must not on either.
    """
    if not std.fs.exists(registry_path(pool_dir)):
        return {"version": _REGISTRY_VERSION, "slots": {}, "absent": True}
    raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(registry_path(pool_dir)) or ""

    # Written by rename, so an empty file is damage as much as garbage is.
    parsed = json.try_decode(raw) if raw.strip() else None
    if parsed == None or type(parsed) != "dict" or type(parsed.get("slots")) != "dict":
        _warn_once(std, pool_dir, "worktree registry at " + pool_dir + " is unreadable, so its slots are not listed; " +
                                  "the next command that changes this pool rebuilds it from git, keeping every slot " +
                                  "git still lists and forgetting the free ones")
        return {"version": _REGISTRY_VERSION, "slots": {}, "unreadable": True}

    version = parsed.get("version", _REGISTRY_VERSION)
    if type(version) != "int" or version < 1:
        _warn_once(std, pool_dir, "worktree registry at " + pool_dir + " records a version no aspect CLI writes, so " +
                                  "it is read as unreadable; the next command that changes this pool rebuilds it from git")
        return {"version": _REGISTRY_VERSION, "slots": {}, "unreadable": True}
    newer = version > _REGISTRY_VERSION
    if newer:
        parsed["read_only"] = True

    # A slot id becomes a path that is deleted, so one that could name anything
    # outside the pool — `..`, a separator — is dropped rather than trusted. An
    # entry of the wrong shape is dropped too, or every command would fail on it.
    for slot_id in list(parsed["slots"].keys()):
        slot = parsed["slots"][slot_id]
        shaped = _safe_slot_id(slot_id) and type(slot) == "dict" and type(slot.get("state")) == "string"
        known = shaped and slot["state"] in [FREE, LEASED, RESERVED]
        if newer and shaped and not known:
            continue
        if not known:
            _warn_once(
                std,
                pool_dir + "\0" + str(slot_id),
                "worktree registry at " + pool_dir + " has an unusable slot entry %r; ignoring it" % slot_id,
            )
            parsed["slots"].pop(slot_id)
            continue
        for key, kind, default in _SLOT_FIELDS:
            if key in slot and type(slot[key]) != kind:
                slot[key] = default
        slot["history"] = [_clean_entry(e) for e in slot.get("history", []) if type(e) == "dict"]
        if type(slot.get("lease")) == "dict":
            slot["lease"] = _clean_entry(slot["lease"])
        elif slot["state"] == LEASED:
            slot["lease"] = {"name": "", "since": 0, "agent": {}}
        else:
            slot["lease"] = None
        if type(slot.get("reservation")) == "dict":
            slot["reservation"] = _clean_entry(slot["reservation"])
        elif slot["state"] == RESERVED:
            slot["state"] = FREE
            slot["reservation"] = None
        else:
            slot["reservation"] = None
    return parsed

# The fields of a lease, a reservation or a history entry, and of the agent
# record inside one, with the type each is read as and its reset value.
_ENTRY_FIELDS = [
    ("name", "string", ""),
    ("token", "string", ""),
    ("pid", "int", 0),
    ("started_at", "string", ""),
    ("since", "int", 0),
    ("leased_ms", "int", 0),
    ("released_ms", "int", 0),
    ("detached", "string", ""),
    ("base", "string", ""),
    ("base_sha", "string", ""),
]
_AGENT_FIELDS = [
    ("kind", "string", ""),
    ("id", "string", ""),
    ("parent_id", "string", ""),
    ("id_source", "string", ""),
    ("harness_id", "string", ""),
    ("pid", "int", 0),
    ("pid_start", "string", ""),
    ("cwd", "string", ""),
    ("project", "string", ""),
    ("source", "string", ""),
    ("seen_ms", "int", 0),
]

def _clean_entry(entry: dict) -> dict:
    """`entry` with every field of the wrong type reset, its agent included."""
    for key, kind, default in _ENTRY_FIELDS:
        if key in entry and type(entry[key]) != kind:
            entry[key] = default
    agent = entry.get("agent")
    agent = agent if type(agent) == "dict" else {}
    for key, kind, default in _AGENT_FIELDS:
        if key in agent and type(agent[key]) != kind:
            agent[key] = default
    entry["agent"] = agent
    return entry

# The fields a slot entry is read as, with the type each must have and what a
# wrong-typed one is reset to.
_SLOT_FIELDS = [
    ("owner_git_dir", "string", ""),
    ("last_branch", "string", ""),
    ("last_commit", "string", ""),
    ("lock_digest", "string", ""),
    ("created_ms", "int", 0),
    ("lease_count", "int", 0),
    ("had_base", "bool", False),
    ("history", "list", []),
]

def _safe_slot_id(slot_id) -> bool:
    """Whether `slot_id` is a single, plain path component."""
    if type(slot_id) != "string" or not slot_id:
        return False
    for ch in slot_id.elems():
        if not (ch.isalnum() or ch in "-_"):
            return False
    return True

def write_registry(std, pool_dir: str, registry: dict) -> None:
    """Replace the registry atomically, so a crash mid-write cannot leave a
    half-written file where the pool's state should be. Refused for a registry
    a newer CLI wrote, which this one would rewrite without what it does not
    understand."""
    if registry.get("read_only", False):
        fail("the worktree registry at " + pool_dir + " was written by a newer aspect CLI; run this " +
             "command with that version, or upgrade, rather than let this one rewrite it")
    tmp = registry_path(pool_dir) + ".tmp"
    kept = {k: v for k, v in registry.items() if k not in ["absent", "unreadable", "read_only"]}
    std.fs.write(tmp, json.encode(kept, indent = 2) + "\n")
    std.fs.rename(tmp, registry_path(pool_dir))

def new_slot_record(pool: dict) -> dict:
    """A fresh slot's registry entry: owned by this clone, never used yet.

    `created_ms` is a floor for `slot_idle_days`, not a record of use. A slot
    nothing has built in yet has no Bazel state to date, and without the floor
    it would read as infinitely old and be pruned before its first build
    finished.
    """
    return {
        "owner_git_dir": pool["git_common_dir"],
        "state": FREE,
        "last_commit": "",
        "last_branch": "",
        "lock_digest": "",
        "created_ms": time.now_ms(),
        "lease_count": 0,
        # Whether an output base has ever been seen for this slot, recorded by
        # `add` and `release`. A lease count cannot stand in for it: a slot
        # leased twice without a build never had a base.
        "had_base": False,
        "history": [],
    }

def _append_holder(slot: dict, name: str, agent: dict) -> None:
    """Add an open history entry naming who is taking the slot.

    The holder keeps its kind, its session id — and, for a subagent, the session
    it ran in — and the directory it ran in, and nothing else. A finished
    session's pid is not evidence of anything and keeping it would invite
    reading it as though it were; the directory is a different kind of fact —
    harnesses file a session under the project it was opened in, so it is what
    lets a past holder's session be named again.
    """
    history = (slot.get("history") or []) + [{
        "name": name,
        "agent": {
            "kind": agent.get("kind", ""),
            "id": agent.get("id", ""),
            "parent_id": agent.get("parent_id", ""),
            "id_source": agent.get("id_source", ""),
            "harness_id": agent.get("harness_id", ""),
            "cwd": agent.get("cwd", ""),
        } if agent else {},
        "leased_ms": time.now_ms(),
        "released_ms": 0,
    }]
    slot["history"] = history[-_HISTORY_LIMIT:]

def record_lease(registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str, agent: dict) -> None:
    """Count a worktree created in this slot, and record who took it.

    Counted per checkout rather than per holder, which is what makes the count
    a measure of reuse: it is how many cold starts this slot has saved.
    """
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    slot["lease_count"] = slot.get("lease_count", 0) + 1
    _append_holder(slot, name, agent)

def record_handover(registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str, agent: dict) -> None:
    """Record a second holder taking over the checkout already in this slot.

    No lease is counted — the slot did not turn over, and nothing was rebuilt —
    but a different session is now responsible for it, which is the question the
    history exists to answer.
    """
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    close_lease(registry, slot_id)
    _append_holder(slot, name, agent)

def held_for_ms(entry: dict) -> int:
    """How long a history entry's holder had the slot, so far if it still does."""
    started = entry.get("leased_ms", 0)
    if not started:
        return 0
    return (entry.get("released_ms", 0) or time.now_ms()) - started

def close_lease(registry: dict, slot_id: str) -> None:
    """Close the slot's open history entry, if it has one.

    Tolerates there being none, which is what a slot freed twice looks like.
    """
    history = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("history") or []
    if history and not history[-1].get("released_ms", 0):
        history[-1]["released_ms"] = time.now_ms()

def mark_free(registry: dict, slot_id: str) -> str:
    """Record that a slot's lease has ended, returning the branch it held.
    Mutates `registry`.

    The bookkeeping half of a lease ending, shared by every way one can end: a
    `release` drops the worktree first, reclaiming an abandoned lease or freeing
    a slot drops it alongside, and `reconcile` finds that git has already lost
    it. The branch held becomes `last_branch`, so the
    slot still scores well for that branch's next `add` — the Bazel state it
    keeps is specific to what was built in it, and that is the whole reason to
    keep the slot. `reservation` is cleared alongside `lease` so that a slot
    never carries either one unless its state says it should.

    The commit is not set here: only a caller that still has the worktree can
    read it, and `release` is the one that does.
    """
    close_lease(registry, slot_id)
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    held = (slot.get("lease") or {}).get("name", "")
    slot["state"] = FREE
    slot["lease"] = None
    slot["reservation"] = None
    if held:
        slot["last_branch"] = held
    return held

def observe_base(registry: dict, slot_id: str, present: bool) -> None:
    """Remember that this slot has had a Bazel output base. Mutates `registry`.

    Once seen, never unseen: a base that exists now and is gone later is the
    one thing worth reporting about it, and a slot that never had one must not
    be told its state was taken. So this only ever sets the flag.

    Called wherever a base is looked at: `add` and `release` persist it, and a
    listing uses it for its own output. A lease count cannot stand in for the
    fact, and recording it only when a lease ends would miss every slot whose
    leases ended some other way.
    """
    if present and not registry["slots"][slot_id].get("had_base", False):
        registry["slots"][slot_id]["had_base"] = True

def mark_leased(registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str, agent: dict) -> None:
    """Record that a slot holds a worktree for `name`, taken by `agent`.
    Mutates `registry`.

    The counterpart to `mark_free`, and the only place a lease is written, so a
    lease and the state that admits it cannot drift apart. Any reservation goes:
    one is a promise to produce the worktree that now exists.

    The history entry is the caller's to record first, because only the caller
    knows which kind of claim this is — a fresh checkout counts as a lease, a
    handover of the checkout already there does not.

    `last_commit` and `lock_digest` describe the tree the slot held when it was
    last freed, which is what scoring compares a free slot on. A lease moves the
    tree on, so they are cleared here and recorded again by whatever frees the
    slot with the worktree in hand; a lease that ends without one leaves them
    empty rather than describing a tree the slot no longer has.
    """
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    slot["last_commit"] = ""
    slot["lock_digest"] = ""
    slot["state"] = LEASED
    slot["reservation"] = None
    slot["lease"] = {"name": name, "since": time.now_ms(), "agent": agent}

def slot_age_ms(slot: dict) -> int:
    """How long this slot has existed, in milliseconds.

    Distinct from `slot_idle_ms`, which is idleness and resets whenever Bazel
    touches the slot's output base. This one only ever grows, and is what "how
    long has this slot been around" means.
    """
    created = slot.get("created_ms", 0)
    return (time.now_ms() - created) if created else 0

def slot_created_days(slot: dict) -> int:
    """`slot_age_ms` in whole days, rounded down."""
    return slot_age_ms(slot) // _DAY_MS

def parse_worktree_list(porcelain: str) -> dict:
    """`{path: branch}` from `git worktree list --porcelain`, in git's order and
    spelled as git spells the paths.

    Read as `-z` writes it, each field ended by a NUL, wherever there is one:
    a path may hold a newline, which the line form would split into a second,
    made-up record. The line form is still read, for a git that has no `-z`.

    The branch is "" for a detached head: a slot taken with `--detach`, or one
    somebody detached by hand.
    """
    found = {}
    at = ""
    for line in (porcelain.split("\0") if "\0" in porcelain else porcelain.splitlines()):
        if line.startswith("worktree "):
            at = line[len("worktree "):]
            found[at] = ""
        elif line.startswith("branch refs/heads/") and at:
            found[at] = line[len("branch refs/heads/"):].strip()
    return found

def worktree_porcelain(std, cwd: str) -> str:
    """`git worktree list --porcelain`, NUL-separated where git can (2.36 on),
    and as lines where it cannot, for `parse_worktree_list`."""
    listed = run_git(std, ["worktree", "list", "--porcelain", "-z"], cwd)
    return listed.out if listed.ok else git_out(std, ["worktree", "list", "--porcelain"], cwd)

def _live_worktrees(std, git_common_dir: str) -> dict:
    """`parse_worktree_list` for every worktree of this clone."""
    return parse_worktree_list(worktree_porcelain(std, git_common_dir))

def _is_our_worktree(std, pool: dict, path: str) -> bool:
    """Whether git says a worktree of *this* clone is at `path`.

    Asked only when `git worktree list` named nothing matching a slot, which
    means either the worktree is gone or the two sides spell the same directory
    differently. Asking git from inside the directory settles it without
    comparing paths at all: this answer and `pool["git_common_dir"]` are both
    git's own, so they agree on spelling by construction.
    """
    if not is_directory(std, path):
        return False
    common = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"], path)
    return common != "" and common == pool["git_common_dir"]

def _unreadable_checkout(std, path: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` holds a checkout git cannot read — its `.git` pointing
    at an object store that moved or was re-cloned. Such a checkout may hold
    work nobody can inspect, so a lease on it stands rather than freeing a slot
    that would then be cleared."""
    return _is_checkout(std, path) and not run_git(std, ["rev-parse", "--git-dir"], path).ok

def drop_vanished_leases(std, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> dict:
    """Free every leased slot whose directory is gone — unless git still keeps
    work for it (`vanished_work`), asked of the clone that owns the slot while
    that clone exists. Mutates `registry`.

    The half of `reconcile` that needs no clone of its own, so it can be
    applied to a pool read from anywhere. A lease is a claim on a checkout, and
    a directory that does not exist holds no files — but its owner's git can
    still hold commits only it reached, which are asked of that clone, and kept
    while it says so. A slot whose owning clone is gone has nothing left to ask.
    What it cannot judge is a directory that is still there, which is where git
    is the authority and `reconcile` takes over.
    """
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] == LEASED and not is_directory(std, slot_path(pool, slot_id)):
            owner = slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") or pool["git_common_dir"]
            if owner and std.fs.exists(owner) and vanished_work(std, owner, slot_path(pool, slot_id)):
                continue
            mark_free(registry, slot_id)
    return registry

def reconcile(std, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> dict:
    """Bring `registry` back in line with git, in place, in both directions.

    Git is the authority on whether a worktree exists, so a slot git has
    forgotten is free regardless of what the registry claims — which is what
    recovers from a hand-run `git worktree remove`, with no lease protocol and
    no timeouts. A worktree the *registry* has forgotten is adopted for the same
    reason: an unreadable registry starts over by design, and without this the
    pool would report one of its own slots as a worktree it does not manage.

    A lease is kept only while the checkout is still there and git agrees it is
    a worktree of this clone — or git cannot read it at all, which is no
    evidence the work in it is gone — and it follows the branch git has there: after a
    `git switch` in the slot, the lease is named for the branch now holding the
    work, which is the name every command looks it up and reports it by.

    A missing directory ends a lease whatever git's list says — git keeps
    listing a worktree until something removes it — unless git still keeps
    work for it, which `drop_vanished_leases` asks the owning clone. A directory that is there outranks git's silence about
    it, since the list is matched by path and two
    spellings of one directory would otherwise read as every lease having ended
    — which hands the next caller an occupied slot to force-remove.

    Only slots this clone owns are judged. A `git worktree list` answers for one
    object store, so another clone's lease is invisible here rather than over.
    Reserved slots are left alone: a reservation means the worktree does not
    exist *yet*, so git's silence about it is expected rather than evidence, and
    `reservation_abandoned` is what judges those.

    A pool read with no clone to ask — `git_common_dir` empty — gets only
    `drop_vanished_leases`, the half that needs no clone of its own.
    """
    drop_vanished_leases(std, pool, registry)
    if not pool["git_common_dir"]:
        return registry
    live = _live_worktrees(std, pool["git_common_dir"])
    slots = registry["slots"]

    for slot_id in slots:
        slot = slots[slot_id]
        if slot["state"] != LEASED:
            continue
        if slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            continue
        path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
        if path in live:
            _follow_switch(slot["lease"], live[path])
            continue
        if _is_our_worktree(std, pool, path) or _unreadable_checkout(std, path):
            continue
        mark_free(registry, slot_id)

    # The other direction: a worktree in one of this clone's slots that the
    # registry does not account for. Leased under the branch git has, with no
    # agent, because who took it is not known.
    prefix = pool["dir"] + "/" + SLOT_PREFIX
    for path in live:
        if not path.startswith(prefix):
            continue

        # Git lists a worktree whose directory is gone until something removes
        # it, and a missing directory holds nothing to adopt.
        if not is_directory(std, path):
            continue
        slot_id = path[len(prefix):]
        if not _safe_slot_id(slot_id):
            continue
        slot = slots.get(slot_id) or new_slot_record(pool)
        if slot["state"] != FREE or slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            continue
        slots[slot_id] = slot
        mark_leased(registry, slot_id, live[path], {})

        # At least one worktree has been in here, which is more than the zero a
        # new record would claim. The real count went with the registry.
        slot["lease_count"] = max(slot.get("lease_count", 0), 1)
    return registry

def adoptable(std, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The slots `adopt_stranded` would take over for this clone, without
    taking them.

    A free slot recorded for a clone that no longer exists at its path: it has
    no checkout to belong to anybody, so any clone of the repository can have
    it, warm base and all — a deleted clone's as much as a moved one's.

    A leased slot whose checkout this clone's git lists and the recorded
    clone's does not: the moved clone's own. Asked of git rather than of the
    path, because a clone moved away and another cloned at its old path leaves
    the path existing and the slots still the moved clone's — and the new
    clone, whose git has never heard of them, must not act on them as its own.

    Empty for a pool read without a clone.
    """
    here = pool["git_common_dir"]
    if not here:
        return []
    lists = {}
    found = []
    for slot_id in sorted(registry["slots"].keys()):
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        owner = slot.get("owner_git_dir", "")
        if not owner or owner == here:
            continue
        if slot["state"] == FREE:
            if not std.fs.exists(owner):
                found.append(slot_id)
            continue
        if slot["state"] != LEASED:
            continue
        path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
        if here not in lists:
            lists[here] = _live_worktrees(std, here)
        if path not in lists[here]:
            continue
        if std.fs.exists(owner):
            if owner not in lists:
                lists[owner] = _live_worktrees(std, owner)
            if path in lists[owner]:
                continue
        found.append(slot_id)
    return found

def adopt_stranded(std, pool: dict, registry: dict, repair: bool = True) -> list[str]:
    """Take over, for this clone, the `adoptable` slots — usually this very
    clone's, moved or renamed. Returns the ids that changed hands, for the
    caller to write and report. Mutates `registry`.

    `git worktree repair` points each leased checkout back at this clone, so
    the lease and its work carry on here; one whose repair fails stays where it
    was, since a lease on a checkout this clone cannot read is not this
    clone's. Repairing changes git, so it is for commands holding the pool
    lock. `repair = False` only re-records the owners, for a preview of what
    such a command would do.
    """
    taken = []
    for slot_id in adoptable(std, pool, registry):
        if repair and registry["slots"][slot_id]["state"] == LEASED:
            if not run_git(std, ["worktree", "repair", slot_path(pool, slot_id)], pool["git_common_dir"]).ok:
                continue
        registry["slots"][slot_id]["owner_git_dir"] = pool["git_common_dir"]
        taken.append(slot_id)
    return taken

def unregister_vanished(std, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> None:
    """Remove from git's list every slot of this pool whose directory is gone.

    A lease's lock keeps git listing such a worktree through any
    `git worktree prune`, so its branch would stay checked out somewhere that
    does not exist and no worktree could take it. This changes git, so it is
    for commands that hold the pool lock, not for `list`, which only reads. A
    slot being set up is left alone: its checkout may not have reached the
    disk yet. So is one git still keeps work for (`vanished_work`), whose
    registration is the last thing holding it.
    """
    prefix = pool["dir"] + "/" + SLOT_PREFIX
    for path in _live_worktrees(std, pool["git_common_dir"]):
        if not path.startswith(prefix) or is_directory(std, path):
            continue
        slot = registry["slots"].get(path[len(prefix):]) or {}
        if slot.get("state", FREE) == RESERVED or vanished_work(std, pool["git_common_dir"], path):
            continue
        catch(run_git, std, FORCE_REMOVE + [path], pool["git_common_dir"])

def _follow_switch(lease: dict, branch: str) -> None:
    """Rename `lease` for `branch`, the branch git has checked out in its slot,
    when the two differ. Mutates `lease`.

    What the lease said about its own start goes with the old name: `detached`
    no longer holds once HEAD is on a branch, and `base` describes where the
    old branch began, not the new one. A detached HEAD (`branch` "") leaves
    the lease as it is.
    """
    if not branch or branch == lease.get("name", ""):
        return
    lease["name"] = branch
    for key in ["detached", "base", "base_sha"]:
        lease.pop(key, None)

def slot_path(pool: dict, slot_id: str) -> str:
    return pool["dir"] + "/" + SLOT_PREFIX + slot_id

def output_base_of(std, slot: str, root: str = "") -> str:
    """The output base Bazel will use for the worktree at `slot`.

    Bazel derives it as `<output_user_root>/md5(workspace_path)` and nothing
    overrides it here, so this is a calculation rather than a choice — which is
    what lets the pool look at a slot's Bazel state without starting a server
    to ask where it is.

    `root` is the output user root to derive under. Callers pass the one the rc
    chain resolves, because a repo that sets `startup --output_user_root=` keeps
    its bases somewhere other than the platform default and a slot looked up in
    the wrong root reads as cold forever. Empty falls back to the platform
    default, which is right outside a workspace and in tests.
    """
    h = md5()
    h.update(slot)
    return (root or output_user_root(std)) + "/" + h.hexdigest()

def server_pid_of(std, slot: str, root: str = "") -> int:
    """PID of the Bazel server holding `slot`'s output base, or 0.

    A recorded pid outlives its process, so a caller that acts on this has to
    check the process too; `warmth_of` does.
    """
    txt = std.fs.try_read_to_string(output_base_of(std, slot, root) + "/server/server.pid.txt").strip()
    if not txt:
        return 0
    for ch in txt.elems():
        if ch < "0" or ch > "9":
            return 0
    return int(txt)

def warmth_of(std, slot: str, root: str = "") -> struct:
    """What a caller would actually gain by taking this slot.

    `server` is a live Bazel server still holding the analysis cache — the
    expensive thing, and the difference between a warm build and a cold one.
    `base` is whether the output base exists at all, which is what makes
    `external/` already materialized. Reported rather than asserted: a slot
    whose server has since exited is still useful, but less so, and saying
    "warm" of it would be a lie.
    """
    base = output_base_of(std, slot, root)
    if not std.fs.exists(base):
        return struct(server = 0, base = False)
    pid = server_pid_of(std, slot, root)
    return struct(server = pid if _is_bases_server(std, pid, base) else 0, base = True)

def _is_bases_server(std, pid: int, base: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `pid` may be `base`'s Bazel server — stricter than `aspect gc`
    about keeping a base, since this one also guards a slot's checkout.

    A recorded pid outlives its process and the OS reuses it, so a live pid is
    not enough. It is ruled out only when its command line names a different
    `--output_base=`; one that names this base, names none, or that `ps`
    cannot read, counts, since keeping a base is the safe answer. The value is
    read up to the next ` --`, so a root with a space in it compares whole.
    """
    if pid <= 0 or not alive(std, pid):
        return False
    out = std.process.command("ps").args(["-p", str(pid), "-o", "command="]).stdout("piped").stderr("null").spawn().wait_with_output()
    flag = " --output_base="
    command = " " + out.stdout.strip()
    if out.status.code != 0 or flag not in command:
        return True
    named = command.partition(flag)[2].partition(" --")[0].strip().rstrip("/")
    return named.rpartition("/")[2] == base.rpartition("/")[2]

def lock_digest(std, cwd: str) -> str:
    """A digest of `MODULE.bazel.lock`, the strongest predictor of whether a
    slot's `external/` tree is reusable.

    An identical lock means identical `generatedRepoSpecs`, so every bzlmod repo
    hashes to the same entry in Bazel's shared repo-contents cache. It does not
    promise a hit — a repo rule that read an env var can still miss on its
    recorded inputs — which is why this scores rather than decides.

    Trailing whitespace is normalized away so that this and `lock_digest_at`,
    which reads through git and cannot preserve it, agree on identical content.
    """
    raw = std.fs.try_read_to_string(cwd + "/MODULE.bazel.lock")
    if not raw:
        return ""
    return _digest(raw.strip())

def lock_digest_at(std, git_common_dir: str, ref: str) -> str:
    """`lock_digest` for the tree at `ref` rather than a checked-out one.

    Allocation has to score against what the slot will hold, which is `ref`, not
    whatever the caller's own working tree happens to be on. Reading the
    checkout instead mislabels every `--create=<ref>` that leaves the current
    branch, and a dirty lock file mislabels even the simple case.

    "" when the ref has no lock file, which `default_score` reads as "unknown"
    rather than "mismatched".
    """
    raw = git_out(std, ["show", ref + ":MODULE.bazel.lock"], git_common_dir)
    if not raw:
        return ""
    return _digest(raw)

def reserve_slot(std, registry: dict, slot_id: str, name: str, agent: dict = {}) -> str:
    """Claim a slot for `name` before its worktree exists, returning the token
    that proves the claim. Mutates `registry`; the caller writes it.

    This is what lets the pool lock be dropped for the checkout: the slot is off
    the table for every other caller, and the record says which process owes the
    pool a worktree, so being slow is distinguishable from being dead.
    """
    record = owner_record(std)
    registry["slots"][slot_id]["state"] = RESERVED

    # The agent as well as the process. A reservation is the one window where
    # two agents can be racing for the same branch, so it is exactly when
    # saying who is doing it is worth something — and the lease that would
    # carry it does not exist yet.
    registry["slots"][slot_id]["reservation"] = record | {
        "name": name,
        "since": time.now_ms(),
        "agent": agent,
    }
    return record["token"]

def reserving_slot(registry: dict, name: str, owner: str) -> str:
    """The slot an in-flight `add` in the clone whose git dir is `owner` has
    claimed for `name`, or "". Branches are per clone, so another clone's
    reservation of the same name is no claim on this one's.

    A branch being checked out is already taken, even though no lease exists
    yet. Without this a second caller asking for the same branch would pick a
    different slot and then have git refuse the checkout — the same answer,
    arriving as a failure rather than a refusal, after a slot's previous
    occupant had been force-removed to make room.
    """
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] != RESERVED or slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") != owner:
            continue
        if (slot.get("reservation") or {}).get("name", "") == name:
            return slot_id
    return ""

def reservation_abandoned(std, registry: dict, slot_id: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a reservation's process is gone, so the slot can be taken back.

    There is no judgement call here, unlike `abandoned_lease`: a reservation
    means the worktree was still being created, so there is no session to resume
    and nothing uncommitted to lose. A reservation that records no usable
    process is treated as abandoned rather than holding a slot forever.
    """
    return not _held_by_a_live_process(std, registry["slots"][slot_id].get("reservation") or {})

def holds_reservation(registry: dict, slot_id: str, token: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `slot_id` is still reserved by the caller holding `token`.

    Checked before a reservation is turned into a lease, because the lock was
    not held in between and the only honest basis for writing the slot is that
    it is still ours.
    """
    if slot_id not in registry["slots"]:
        return False
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    if slot["state"] != RESERVED:
        return False
    return (slot.get("reservation") or {}).get("token", "") == token

def owned_slots(pool: dict, registry: dict) -> list[str]:
    """Slot ids this clone owns, in registry order. The only slots it can lease,
    and so the only ones its `max_slots` budget counts."""
    return [
        slot_id
        for slot_id in registry["slots"]
        if registry["slots"][slot_id].get("owner_git_dir", "") == pool["git_common_dir"]
    ]

def slot_idle_ms(std, pool: dict, slot_id: str, slot: dict, root: str = "") -> int:
    """How long since the slot was last used: the newer of Bazel's last activity
    in its output base and its last lease, taken or ended. `list` shows it as
    `last used`, and `prune` acts on it.

    Bazel's side is read from the base's own freshness markers, the evidence
    `aspect gc` ages a base on. A lease counts too, since the slot just given
    back is the one most likely wanted again whether or not it built anything;
    `gc` may still collect such a base, which leaves the slot cold rather than
    gone. The slot's creation is the floor beneath both, and the only evidence
    for a slot that has held nothing.
    """
    newest = max(last_used(std, output_base_of(std, slot_path(pool, slot_id), root)) // 1000, slot.get("created_ms", 0))
    for entry in slot.get("history") or []:
        newest = max(newest, entry.get("leased_ms", 0), entry.get("released_ms", 0))
    return (time.now_ms() - newest) if newest else 0

def slot_idle_days(std, pool: dict, slot_id: str, slot: dict, root: str = "") -> int:
    """`slot_idle_ms` in whole days, rounded down. What `prune` acts on."""
    return slot_idle_ms(std, pool, slot_id, slot, root) // _DAY_MS

def prunable_slots(std, pool: dict, registry: dict, max_idle_days: int, root: str = "", stranded_at_once: bool = True) -> list[str]:
    """Free slots whose Bazel state has gone cold enough to be worth less than
    the disk it holds.

    Only free slots are returned, whatever the ages involved. A lease means
    someone may be working in that checkout right now, and a timer is not
    grounds for deleting it; a reservation means a checkout is being created in
    that very directory, and deleting the output base out from under it would
    break a command that is still running.

    A slot owned by *another* clone is returned only once that clone's object
    store is gone from this machine, and so only where `adopt_stranded` has not
    run first — outside any clone, where nothing is adopted. Inside one, such a
    free slot has been taken over and ages as this clone's own. A second live
    clone's slots are none of our business.

    Stranded slots skip the age rule, because that rule weighs Bazel state
    worth keeping against the disk it costs and a stranded slot read from
    outside every clone has nothing on the first side of it. Waiting
    `PRUNE_IDLE_DAYS` to collect one is all cost and no benefit — when
    somebody asked. `stranded_at_once = False` makes a stranded slot wait out
    the idle period like any other, for the sweep nobody asked for: an owner
    that does not exist *here* may be on a volume that is not mounted, or a
    container sharing this home, and is not shown to be gone.
    """
    victims = []
    for slot_id in registry["slots"]:
        slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
        if slot["state"] != FREE:
            continue

        # Residue: an entry with no recorded age, no output base and no
        # directory has nothing behind it to protect, and nothing to age it out
        # by either — so without this it would sit in the registry forever. A
        # registry edited by hand is the way to get one.
        if not slot.get("created_ms", 0):
            path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
            if not std.fs.exists(path) and not std.fs.exists(output_base_of(std, path, root)):
                victims.append(slot_id)
                continue

        # Another clone's slot: none of our business while that clone exists,
        # and collectable at once when it does not. Asked before the age rule,
        # which has nothing to weigh for a slot that can never be leased again.
        owner = slot.get("owner_git_dir", "")
        if owner and owner != pool["git_common_dir"]:
            if not std.fs.exists(owner) and (stranded_at_once or slot_idle_days(std, pool, slot_id, slot, root) >= max_idle_days):
                victims.append(slot_id)
            continue

        if slot_idle_days(std, pool, slot_id, slot, root) < max_idle_days:
            continue
        victims.append(slot_id)
    return victims

def lease_last_active_ms(std, pool: dict, slot_id: str, slot: dict, root: str = "") -> int:
    """When anything last happened in a leased slot, in ms: the lease being
    taken, Bazel using the slot's output base, or git writing the checkout's
    index or HEAD. 0 when none of it can be read.

    The pool records no moment a session stopped — only that its process is
    gone — so this is the clock a stopped session's grace period runs on.
    """
    lease = slot.get("lease") or {}
    since = lease.get("since", 0)
    newest = since if type(since) == "int" else (int(since) if type(since) == "string" and since.isdigit() else 0)
    path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
    base_used = last_used(std, output_base_of(std, path, root)) // 1000
    newest = max(newest, base_used)
    git_dir = git_out(std, ["rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir"], path) if _is_checkout(std, path) else ""
    for marker in ["index", "HEAD", "logs/HEAD"]:
        err, meta = catch(std.fs.metadata, git_dir + "/" + marker) if git_dir else ("", None)
        if err == None and meta != None and meta.modified:
            newest = max(newest, meta.modified // 1000)
    return newest

def abandoned_lease(
        std,
        pool: dict,
        registry: dict,
        slot_id: str,
        root: str = "",
        grace_ms: int = 0) -> bool:
    """Whether a leased slot can be taken back without losing anything.

    **A clean checkout is the condition that never bends.** Commits survive
    `git worktree remove` — they are in the branch — so a checkout with nothing
    in `work_in` has nothing to lose: no uncommitted changes, no commits only a
    detached HEAD reached. Files git ignores are disposable. No amount of elapsed
    time makes deleting work the right default, so a slot holding any is never
    taken however old. Bazel's own leavings are not work, or a slot that had
    been built in could never be reclaimed, which is every slot the pool is for.

    Given that, the other conditions are that the holder is verifiably not
    there — a recorded process that is not running — and has been given
    `grace_ms` since the slot's last activity to come back. A live holder keeps
    its slot however long since its last build, because that measures Bazel rather than
    whether somebody is reading code. So does a holder whose process cannot be
    checked at all — no pid recorded — since that is no evidence it has gone.

    The grace runs from the slot's last activity rather than from the process
    stopping, which nothing records: a session closed mid-task and resumed the
    next morning finds its slot. And it is only asked at the point of need — by
    `_pick_slot` once no free slot has scored and before it grows the pool, and
    by `add` when the branch asked for is the one the abandoned lease holds —
    so nothing is reclaimed when nobody wants a slot.

    `root` is the output user root the rc chain resolves; without it a relocated
    base cannot be recognized and Bazel's leavings would read as work.
    """
    slot = registry["slots"][slot_id]
    lease = slot.get("lease") or {}
    agent = lease.get("agent") or {}

    # No agent recorded, no evidence. The rule is that the holder's process is
    # verifiably gone, and verifying that needs to know what the process was —
    # so a lease nothing identifies is left alone rather than guessed about. A
    # person's worktree is theirs until they release it, and unlike a crashed
    # agent a person can see it in `list` and do so.
    if not agent:
        return False

    # Nor does a holder whose process was never captured: an explicit kind
    # drops the detected pid, and a script can name a session with no harness
    # behind it. Not running cannot be told from not checkable there.
    if not agent.get("pid", 0):
        return False

    # A live holder keeps its slot.
    if is_running(std, agent):
        return False

    # A session that stopped is given `grace_ms` from the slot's last activity
    # to come back to it — resumed, or restarted on its branch — before the
    # slot is anybody else's.
    if grace_ms > 0 and time.now_ms() - lease_last_active_ms(std, pool, slot_id, slot, root) < grace_ms:
        return False

    # Below here the holder is not there, and only the checkout decides — or,
    # with it gone, what git still keeps for it.
    path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
    owner = slot.get("owner_git_dir", "") or pool["git_common_dir"]
    return not work_in(std, path, output_base_of(std, path, root), owner)

def lock_reason(slot_id: str) -> str:
    """The reason a leased slot's git lock gives, which is what a plain
    `git worktree remove` of it prints when refusing. Named by slot, since
    `git switch` in the slot changes its branch and the lock is not rewritten."""
    return "leased from the aspect worktree pool; `aspect worktree release " + slot_id + "` ends it"

def remove_checkout(std, path: str, owner: str) -> bool:
    """Remove the checkout at `path`, returning whether it is gone.

    The owning clone's git is asked to unregister the worktree when that clone
    still exists, and the directory is deleted whatever git says. A checkout git
    does not recognise — left by a clone that was deleted, or one deleted and
    cloned again at the same path — has nobody to ask, and leaving it would
    make its slot unallocatable. Safe on a path whose directory is already gone:
    git still lists a locked worktree there until it is removed by name. A
    read-only tree is made writable first, as `remove_tree` does, and a plain
    file where the directory should be is removed too — before git is asked,
    since git will not unregister a worktree whose path is a file, and keeps
    it locked.

    No work check is made here: a caller decides that first, with `work_in`
    naming the clone, as every one does.
    """
    err, meta = catch(std.fs.symlink_metadata, path)
    if err == None and (meta.is_symlink or not is_directory(std, path)):
        catch(std.fs.remove_file, path)
    if owner and std.fs.exists(owner):
        catch(run_git, std, FORCE_REMOVE + [path], owner)
    if is_directory(std, path):
        remove_tree(std, path)

        # Git would not let go while the directory was there; with it gone, it
        # does. By name, never `git worktree prune`, which would also drop the
        # admin directory of every other worktree of the clone whose directory
        # is missing — a moved one's commits included.
        if owner and std.fs.exists(owner):
            catch(run_git, std, FORCE_REMOVE + [path], owner)
    gone, _ = catch(std.fs.symlink_metadata, path)
    return gone != None

def drop_slot(std, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str, root: str = "", discard: bool = False) -> struct:
    """Delete a slot outright — a free one, or a stranded one named to `prune`:
    its worktree, its directory, its output base and its registry entry. Mutates
    `registry`; the caller writes it.

    A slot still held by a live Bazel server is refused rather than deleted.
    Unlinking an output base from under a running server is how you get a
    process writing into directories that no longer exist, and a free slot with
    a live server is the warmest thing in the pool anyway — exactly what the
    pool is for. `aspect worktree list` reports it, and it becomes prunable on
    its own once Bazel's idle timeout fires.

    So is a slot whose checkout holds work (`work_in`), unless `discard` says
    the caller saw it and chose to: a free slot should hold none, and when it
    does the bookkeeping is what is wrong, not the work.

    Removing the base here rather than leaving it for `aspect gc` is deliberate:
    a prune that reclaimed no disk and told the user to go run something else
    would not be a prune.
    """
    path = slot_path(pool, slot_id)
    base = output_base_of(std, path, root)
    warmth = warmth_of(std, path, root)
    if warmth.server:
        return struct(dropped = False, reason = "a Bazel server (pid %d) still holds it" % warmth.server)
    owner = registry["slots"][slot_id].get("owner_git_dir", "") or pool["git_common_dir"]
    work = [] if discard else work_in(std, path, base, owner if std.fs.exists(owner) else "")
    if work:
        more = (" and %d more" % (len(work) - 1)) if len(work) > 1 else ""
        return struct(dropped = False, reason = "its checkout holds work that would be lost: " + work[0] + more)

    base_err = remove_tree(std, base)
    if base_err:
        return struct(dropped = False, reason = "its output base could not be removed: " + base_err)

    # Kept on the books while its directory is still on disk, or nothing would
    # ever look at that directory again.
    if not remove_checkout(std, path, owner):
        return struct(dropped = False, reason = "its directory could not be removed: " + path)
    registry["slots"].pop(slot_id)
    return struct(dropped = True, reason = "")

def free_slot(std, pool: dict, registry: dict, slot_id: str) -> None:
    """Give a slot back, keeping it and its output base. Mutates `registry`.

    Only the worktree goes, which is what makes this different from
    `drop_slot`: the Bazel state is the reason the pool exists, and a claim
    ending is no reason to throw it away.

    Dropping the worktree here rather than leaving it for the next
    `git worktree add` keeps the branch out of `git worktree list` — otherwise
    the next request for that branch would report it as checked out somewhere
    the pool does not manage, which is both wrong and the opposite of helpful.
    A slot whose directory has already gone is unregistered from git here
    too: git would otherwise refuse to check the branch out again while it
    still lists the worktree, which it does for as long as the lease's lock
    stands.
    """
    remove_checkout(std, slot_path(pool, slot_id), pool["git_common_dir"])
    mark_free(registry, slot_id)

def new_slot_id(std, pool: dict, registry: dict) -> str:
    """An id no slot in this pool is using.

    Each attempt digests something different: the clock alone does not move
    between attempts in one millisecond, so a collision would otherwise repeat
    on every try.
    """
    for attempt in range(_SLOT_ID_ATTEMPTS):
        seed = "%s/%d/%d/%d" % (pool["id"], time.now_ms(), len(registry["slots"]), attempt)
        candidate = _digest(seed)[:_SLOT_ID_LENGTH]
        if candidate not in registry["slots"]:
            return candidate
    fail("could not allocate a worktree slot id")

def default_score(slot: dict, want: dict) -> int:
    """How good a reuse `slot` is for `want`. Higher wins; 0 refuses.

    `slot` is what its last occupant left behind, so its commit is keyed
    `last_commit`; `want` describes the worktree about to be created, so its
    commit is keyed `commit`, and its branch `last_branch` against `want`'s
    `name`. The lock digest is keyed the same on both sides.

    The ordering follows how much of the previous build survives, highest first:

      same commit    warm, and the tree is identical, so the outputs are
                     exactly right
      same branch    warm, with outputs for a near relative of this tree, which
                     is what an agent returning to a branch it released wants
      neither        warm, from some other work at some other point
      lock differs   warm, but a different `MODULE.bazel.lock` means the repo
                     fetches miss and `external/` is rebuilt — the expensive
                     part, though the action cache still counts for something
      cold           no output base now (`base_present`, which the caller fills
                     in), so nothing survives whatever the slot last held

    Every free slot stays eligible — a cold one, a new one included, still
    beats creating a slot — so nothing here returns 0, which refuses.
    """
    if not slot.get("base_present", False):
        return _SCORE_COLD
    if slot.get("lock_digest", "") and want.get("lock_digest", ""):
        if slot["lock_digest"] != want["lock_digest"]:
            return _SCORE_LOCK_DIFFERS
    if slot.get("last_commit", "") and slot["last_commit"] == want.get("commit", ""):
        return _SCORE_SAME_COMMIT
    if slot.get("last_branch", "") and slot["last_branch"] == want.get("name", ""):
        return _SCORE_SAME_BRANCH
    return _SCORE_WARM

# `default_score`'s ladder, highest first.
_SCORE_SAME_COMMIT = 100
_SCORE_SAME_BRANCH = 95
_SCORE_WARM = 90
_SCORE_LOCK_DIFFERS = 10
_SCORE_COLD = 1
